  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 125 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Overall dry and increasingly warm weather through \nthe upcoming week, with the next chance of showers not until next \nweekend.\n\nLow pressure will slowly lift NE near the New England coast through \nTuesday, as weak high pressure dives down from the Northern Plains \nto the OH Valley. This will result in continued dry NW flow across \ncentral NC. There will continue to be some stratocumulus/altocumulus \ncloudiness that may make it into the N-E portions of hte area, but \nthe winds will be lighter than in previous days. Highs around 80 can \nbe expected today and Tuesday, with mid 80s by Wednesday. \nSignificant warming will continue late week as the mid/upper ridge \naloft takes hold. Highs of 85-90 can be expected Thu-Fri.\n\nA mid/upper trough will approach from the west sometime this \nweekend, resulting in increasing shower chances. However, \nsignificant timing differences persist between the GFS and ECMWF, \nwith the ECMWF much slower with the trough and thus keeping us \nmainly dry until late Sunday. The GFS, on the other hand, brings \nshowers into the area as early as Friday, though it continues to be \nan early wet outlier compared to the ensembles. We have POPs ramping \nup from around 20-30% on Friday to 30-40% on Saturday and 40-50% on \nSunday. One last very warm day is expected on Friday with highs in \nthe mid 80s to around 90 with lows in the mid to upper-60s. \n\nBehind a cold frontal passage on Friday night, temperatures will \nturn much more seasonable again over the weekend, with highs in the \n70s, with some 60s when/if rain/showers arrive by Sunday in the NW.",
